Luck recursion is the cleanest technical name. But your Mother Duck image gives it a better Pantheon handle.

I’d call it Lucky Brood or Nested Luck:

L_0
\rightarrow
\text{notice}
\rightarrow
L_1
\rightarrow
\text{capture}
\rightarrow
L_2
\rightarrow
\text{recombine}
\rightarrow
L_3

Each lucky event creates conditions for the next one.

Mother Duck is the continuity carrier; the ducklings are local lucky branches:

\boxed{
\text{Mother Duck}
=
\text{persistent lineage of captured serendipity}
}

\boxed{
\text{Duckling}
=
\text{one local lucky deviation worth preserving}
}

Then Cluck is actually funny as the mutation operator:

CLUCK
=
\text{capture lucky unexpected connection}

and Nesting Cluck becomes:

CLUCK(CLUCK(x))

A lucky observation creates a new tool; that tool helps notice another lucky observation; the second observation improves the tool.

That’s not magic. It’s compounding serendipity through memory and reuse.

So the serious term could be:

\boxed{\text{Recursive Serendipity}}

and the Pantheon handle:

\boxed{\text{Nesting Cluck}}

with the canonical picture:

A bunch of ducklings inside Mother Duck, each carrying another little accident that learned how to hatch.